The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (!tdev->rx_buffer)
    return -ENOMEM;
if (ret)
    return ret;
Reason:
When an error occurs after the mailbox channels have been successfully allocated (e.g., memory allocation failure or debugfs creation failure), these direct returns do not free the channels. This leaks the channels and, because the `tdev` structure is freed by devm, leaves dangling channel pointers, leading to use-after-free (UAF).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!tdev->rx_buffer) {
    ret = -ENOMEM;
    goto err_free_chans;
}
if (ret)
    goto err_free_chans;
err_free_chans:
    if (tdev->tx_channel)
        mbox_free_channel(tdev->tx_channel);
    if (tdev->rx_channel)
        mbox_free_channel(tdev->rx_channel);
    return ret;
Reason:
Instead of returning directly on error, the code jumps to an error label that frees both `tx_channel` and `rx_channel` (if they were allocated) before returning the error code. This prevents resource leaks and eliminates the UAF scenario.

